#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8dbb426-dd34-5686-bde9-3ef9d246b5e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3beb2bf3-85a6-5351-8707-3d22b1ee479b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348988} {:answer/id #uuid "7bcaac38-7ca5-5639-8065-5ed95845a2c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,1]\\cup [3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349583} {:answer/id #uuid "b9239d6e-0398-536e-96ea-926946d19cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [-1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350087} {:answer/id #uuid "f19cb4c0-bc6d-5293-af8d-6cb78b330c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350559} {:answer/id #uuid "f665cb8d-c8b2-5193-ac92-977971e1d72e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f665cb8d-c8b2-5193-ac92-977971e1d72e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350603} :answer-field/id #uuid "718ade24-34b7-5766-a493-7153cbbe8e60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6209583e-a305-5e36-8803-ab5525f3111a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81568" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5).$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\le -1 \\\\  & 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\le -2 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\ge 1 \\\\  & 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\ge 2 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -2,\\,f(x)\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ec59516-4ba6-5e62-b26a-bad36ea4448f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Asymptotes of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e37e352-2ebd-519b-b752-f0f303d5c735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348712} {:answer/id #uuid "4d2d114b-f236-58ac-bb08-c56f9ad411f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349151} {:answer/id #uuid "76d9e37c-df77-5f7d-b7e9-be9174f15aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349539} {:answer/id #uuid "88cba209-3291-583a-9d5b-5e1a16336323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349689} {:answer/id #uuid "c32d5354-3148-51a9-aede-6809d8386c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e37e352-2ebd-519b-b752-f0f303d5c735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348712} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b4a398d-6bb6-535f-9a7e-aa4d0e416252" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c63c20b6-af06-5453-a6bf-493adf10c2a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81581" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+{30}^{∘}),$ where $x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+{30}^{∘}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(x+{30}^{∘}).$ This gives\n\n$$\nx+{30}^{∘}\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+{30}^{∘} & \\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & \\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘},$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115202 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc0be03-9369-508e-9765-8c88871ec20b" :knowledge-point/title "The Range of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14b7885b-ae95-575e-97f6-8a71e1b57ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404193} {:answer/id #uuid "65627c21-66cb-5f02-8ba6-0c37010c9bc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404194} {:answer/id #uuid "dea138f5-3204-5f95-9262-ff365ea22b31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404195} {:answer/id #uuid "3f211c15-8d28-53f3-8403-b6b4d9ef7e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has no maximum value" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404196} {:answer/id #uuid "21d62bc4-f68d-55ab-b0ca-9dee218d1167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f211c15-8d28-53f3-8403-b6b4d9ef7e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has no maximum value" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404196} :answer-field/id #uuid "64bfa454-0c70-5d48-80bc-d4490bd1867f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b338b5cf-801c-50c4-9622-d9ab0fa4da90" :question/math-academy-id "q-81863" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-3)+2.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ and the function has no maximum value."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115208 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f6f4cfa-db03-5ae6-9f43-79fd3167c473" :knowledge-point/title "The Vertical Asymptote of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "379491ec-d15c-5213-ab37-21be3454aa2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348948} {:answer/id #uuid "5675de99-d640-5a79-8625-1bf0d30b0cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349237} {:answer/id #uuid "a4caf121-6c14-50f0-aeb3-fd2aed32744f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349918} {:answer/id #uuid "f28f2ddb-3aaf-5065-ac5a-85f3f483c5ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350574} {:answer/id #uuid "ff18f0bb-e463-5951-b0c4-3badeb9977f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5675de99-d640-5a79-8625-1bf0d30b0cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349237} :answer-field/id #uuid "6545ad2b-c92a-5487-bc20-82c927dbe614" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e53bfbce-8b32-5854-943b-ffd612dcb153" :question/math-academy-id "q-81994" :question/problem "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x)=-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-2x)+6.$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is $x=0.$\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x),$ we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-2x & =0 \\\\ -2x & =-4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087730 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb2860c1-8ed6-5c0c-8f43-c1ba75225c02" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complex Conjugate of a Complex Number With Positive Imaginary Part"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0922c4b6-d25d-551a-9cb3-5200b81e3ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=3+5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348522} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6579a7-a15a-511f-8185-1f0d53d6b68b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=\\frac{1}{3+5\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349605} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce7bbc3-fac1-53f3-a1de-86a5cc73fcd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=-3-5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349845} {:answer/id #uuid "b477f944-a4de-5199-ad13-36fc4346d627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=-3+5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350042} {:answer/id #uuid "b5672995-afd4-5eed-ad77-cd6033ca9a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=3-5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5672995-afd4-5eed-ad77-cd6033ca9a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{―}{z}=3-5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350052} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b1cebfd-d980-591f-bac4-9d01fdc80d24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8be49ea4-410e-5bb3-9aa7-a4c55fff790b" :question/math-academy-id "q-8220" :question/problem "What is the complex conjugate of $z=3+5\\text{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number $z=3+5\\text{i},$ the imaginary part is $5\\text{i}.$ Flipping the sign, we get $-5\\text{i}.$\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by $\\overset{―}{z}=3-5\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "833ac63c-235b-5059-ab7c-56eb1e44d79e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Second Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bdc6138-01e7-5dca-90c2-ee5ef772f650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348685} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5f7895-83a7-5868-921c-57c50450ef1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348975} {:answer/id #uuid "69962bbe-75b8-5ae4-99aa-4f7910f71965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349425} {:answer/id #uuid "7df6bfdf-a463-5e56-a7b0-8cc61b55f17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.07" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349601} {:answer/id #uuid "b60fe42a-6aaa-59ac-b35e-8ac4d19e26ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bdc6138-01e7-5dca-90c2-ee5ef772f650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348685} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c0ed589-e9f7-57d6-8078-aa521972d708" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61e93f3c-1103-58d6-a5e4-4e5649a9f688" :question/math-academy-id "q-8258" :question/problem "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-2+5\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/fc/fc6a2568ba77ce043ae0d48840ed4707b3e629b8a52c4b5f182c1e23a249fa44.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<\\pi .$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan⁡(\\frac{5}{2}) \\\\  & ≈1.190\\text{ }29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)≈\\pi -1.190\\text{ }29≈1.95\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087718 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a6128-22df-5a02-96fd-7c8cabf8f5f6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Complex Number in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ef259f9-6220-5719-b73f-f9e14cba53a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=13(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.176))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348863} {:answer/id #uuid "305d1949-c98f-5233-bbfc-3a89cbfc0c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=15(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.723)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.723))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348876} {:answer/id #uuid "6a6d4a2e-e6b9-5883-a2d2-4f5ef0dac7a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{15}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.486)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.486))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349428} {:answer/id #uuid "8e3107c4-9eb8-5fe0-9fae-1d7e36aa0717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{13}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.176))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349728} {:answer/id #uuid "b1280a6d-c454-5f65-93e2-6c3b8cbf0d5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=13(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.641)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.641))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ef259f9-6220-5719-b73f-f9e14cba53a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=13(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.176))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348863} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e03d1a2-30fd-5e5d-a7b1-322a0564c17b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d310c8c-3e11-5226-89ae-78e76ac29fd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-8263" :question/problem "Express $z=5+12\\text{i}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give $\\theta $ in radians to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "In polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=5+12\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=5$ is positive and $y=12$ is positive, $z$ lies in the first quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{12}{5}) \\\\  & ≈1.176,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z=5+12\\text{i}$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=13(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.176))\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088417 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "865035a9-dfe6-51bf-a5dc-624240267541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087156} {:answer/id #uuid "981d3229-34da-5547-9622-76e7a57b9b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087157} {:answer/id #uuid "ff08f834-c93c-58b6-a6eb-be4ab302b1ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087158} {:answer/id #uuid "5826ab39-99cb-5f0d-a91a-c8e47d4e7431" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087159} {:answer/id #uuid "58fc334c-401e-58ca-8084-7bbee1c81411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087160}] :answer-field/id #uuid "212166f2-0485-5ec6-aaa6-538998150651" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087155} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9010786-eb95-569b-8bf6-50b7c4d11251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396198} {:answer/id #uuid "9017ed40-b00d-55a3-877f-c3d4b075da7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396199} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a1da3a-abde-5c78-94f6-9beb1ca8472a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396200} {:answer/id #uuid "8a2fc306-e79b-5a44-8145-c511e597d027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396201} {:answer/id #uuid "8b5eb027-48d1-5f9c-9af9-618b997023bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9010786-eb95-569b-8bf6-50b7c4d11251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396198} :answer-field/id #uuid "169533db-26aa-5a83-a7a7-fa42593fd137" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9a96319-3bb3-5dd4-a3ac-27f5effab07d" :question/math-academy-id "q-8331" :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\nII. $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\nIII. $T$ is a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}ky \\\\ kx\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ x\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2785b5bd-88db-544d-a41d-b69d35be99ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348787} {:answer/id #uuid "354f131e-ae5c-51ab-8348-a87034b594f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348929} {:answer/id #uuid "4f24dbbe-b87e-5b97-894f-05bae0099c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349162} {:answer/id #uuid "75e8d951-cd2e-5911-8618-a190ffa28f20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349532} {:answer/id #uuid "faa13be6-1fba-5e10-bc32-18634ce1a08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2785b5bd-88db-544d-a41d-b69d35be99ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348787} :answer-field/id #uuid "74f262d7-769f-525a-a77b-2e82355e8aef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8324b04-6c39-5b50-ae14-3bae7af8be74" :question/math-academy-id "q-83840" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{5}+21{x}^{3}+16x+14,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $14,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 7,$ and $\\pm 14.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{7}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{2}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{14}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{14}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 7, & \\pm \\frac{7}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 14, & \\pm 7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm \\frac{7}{2}\\,\\pm 7,\\,\\pm 14.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c249cf3a-7864-5132-80fa-c18a3ea915c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Intercepts of a Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "336d5b0b-46fb-592f-8727-3a06de946703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352042} {:answer/id #uuid "82209c5f-513d-558a-80b5-81bcad912708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352699} {:answer/id #uuid "da005a29-2e30-58a3-8eda-87359ad503f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pm 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353459} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb5462e-f1c5-5814-83c4-35e425cabf84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pm 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353512} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7b25f7-d0b9-528a-83fb-6c5aa038ad8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no $y$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82209c5f-513d-558a-80b5-81bcad912708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352699} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f23fd7e-f0b2-5902-a559-b924fdec7db9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40af28c7-87f4-525e-8058-edd168d8bbcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-84045" :question/problem "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{25}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0+3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{25} & =0 \\\\ (y-5)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-5 & =0 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $(0,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d21b01-bbad-507d-8b3c-1052ebba30eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22276dc2-a2b9-5cbc-8c67-2986ce0007da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351876} {:answer/id #uuid "3406dbe4-4a00-5248-9893-ce53b859d49c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352051} {:answer/id #uuid "75d0455a-51e7-593c-ac60-e49ce930f87f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352603} {:answer/id #uuid "b754e390-db22-57b2-ae0d-9a1ac89fa09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353135} {:answer/id #uuid "d21008dd-c70e-516f-b57d-efa1aabd8110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b754e390-db22-57b2-ae0d-9a1ac89fa09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353135} :answer-field/id #uuid "b989a899-d8b9-5376-915b-28b5573472a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f063fbc-7799-5914-b601-73bb4ae22301" :question/math-academy-id "q-8431" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$  what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is not in the range $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ To get the first solution that's within the required range, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ To do this, we simply add $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](images/07/0716b8ab50e525f453ae8bc690d404a150f2da98f0a90b6c8856ae9925e7c515.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{4\\pi }{3}=\\frac{20{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "54588722-bb80-54ca-b100-03aca39e0c17" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Fourth Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f1b0500-415f-5429-89d9-fbbb92a98ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352546} {:answer/id #uuid "cd29b07a-70bf-5347-a5b0-93e51c59301b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353348} {:answer/id #uuid "e36a586c-680f-580d-a120-8ca0ede7f5be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353548} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8e504f-d2c6-5f83-8ce5-d298b778f10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353653} {:answer/id #uuid "fde2ebba-1cad-5211-b6e3-3a258bce156d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fde2ebba-1cad-5211-b6e3-3a258bce156d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353793} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ee08007-7eba-5dcc-a203-e575bd3f6bdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1eede2a0-b32f-5395-87d1-df0fef1f0013" :question/math-academy-id "q-8454" :question/problem "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=7-5\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/d0/d096d76118bc06b2d5c79d5017c0178d96fc84bff6f97d563b7b872d26e75d20.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =arctan⁡(\\frac{5}{7})≈0.620\\,25.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)=2\\pi -0.620\\,25≈5.66\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096711 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61861939-69d4-5b1e-a0f0-6a1b383b775b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vertex of a Horizontal Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f2aa742-ff1a-54b2-97a0-0bebb3cecd20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351696} {:answer/id #uuid "1e476e2b-6825-5337-9a24-4ffc405d5de9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351836} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf837f8-b43f-5397-b414-cc6a493481ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352388} {:answer/id #uuid "abe94b80-ee11-5325-9ca6-efb5e6a9e1da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353025} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b8605f-99d6-5803-9576-5d4c433892db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abe94b80-ee11-5325-9ca6-efb5e6a9e1da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353025} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ea64b02-1605-5ead-bad0-1b95b5a94b27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c3b2857-f742-54ff-a16a-524aeaa787ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-84952" :question/problem "![](images/83/83217df9245edba7b09f5b29edd8862564c3c4e772ba2da45e1c4e11f10c7747.png)\n\nWhat is the vertex of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The vertex of a left-opening parabola is the right-most point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/80/80c3336af95cf45198f4d8cf3b8718c7dcd2512f4720948d0edda3d987a6aa0c.png)\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(0,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67455965-c9d3-5ae8-8b56-2a58af95df0a" :knowledge-point/title "Stating the Vertex of a Vertical Parabola Given Its Equation in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1345d968-20d1-5ac9-bbfe-3c46ce781861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351732} {:answer/id #uuid "967290dc-71b3-501d-8605-16ee088f7c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352870} {:answer/id #uuid "a4389b55-ae83-53e1-9f8d-a4182b6742a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{5}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352968} {:answer/id #uuid "c15bc970-f051-5dfb-a7f1-db97122d6816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{4},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353237} {:answer/id #uuid "ded0b80c-41d3-53a3-9730-fea5f6f67367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1345d968-20d1-5ac9-bbfe-3c46ce781861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351732} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7c729ab-9ef7-56a2-b67a-e30b58f4144d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a3d9a2c-1761-5baf-9e5d-4958b26130d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-84954" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${x}^{2}=5y?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4(\\frac{5}{4})y.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{5}{4}.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "120f626e-dd00-537a-8948-d8f81850d696" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "260ee64b-60f9-577f-b9cd-899af296d9b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351917} {:answer/id #uuid "73b8b006-61f8-5c7a-906f-a83360295df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352580} {:answer/id #uuid "b91e1eea-f224-503d-85e7-b2faa41fc99d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353149} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9be550-efdd-5dc5-80d3-353296eb9f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "243" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353327} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9b644a-cfec-5d4f-922b-5a3e24daabe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "260ee64b-60f9-577f-b9cd-899af296d9b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351917} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcee7007-39c0-575f-87b4-92eaeeb2b54a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9b3fff3-7ca8-5a05-bacf-2186c0ce6253" :question/math-academy-id "q-84956" :question/problem "A pizzeria has nine different toppings from which to choose. How many different pizzas can be made using only three toppings?" :question/worked-solution "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $9$ toppings, so $n=9.$ We want to select $3$ of those toppings for each of the pizzas, so we have $r=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different pizzas is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{9}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{9!}{(9-3)!\\,3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9!}{6!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot \\cancel{6!}}{\\cancel{6!}3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{504}{6} \\\\  & =84.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "120f626e-dd00-537a-8948-d8f81850d696" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1755d2b2-8ef2-5841-967a-37231874078e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "154" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351776} {:answer/id #uuid "22aa46f6-d9f6-52a0-921d-e699a6fdabc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351880} {:answer/id #uuid "aefddd11-ffea-5b50-af77-6441d3546955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "102" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353052} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ef2388-0f72-5871-b0be-8276cb5fe915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353453} {:answer/id #uuid "debdfcd2-ba10-5c33-a152-32714bf2926b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "126" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "debdfcd2-ba10-5c33-a152-32714bf2926b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "126" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353500} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb52f3bb-59b4-5a00-86e6-1550bcc92127" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44e1ceb1-ad30-5708-98e7-b7ac9be19ae7" :question/math-academy-id "q-84959" :question/problem "Sara has a box of $9$ colored pencils. In how many ways can Sara pick a set of $4$ different pencils from the box?" :question/worked-solution "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $9$ pencils in the box, so $n=9.$ We want to select $4$ of those pencils, so we have $r=4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different potential sets of four pencils is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{9}{C}_{4} & =\\frac{9!}{(9-4)!\\,4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9!}{5!4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{\\cancel{5!}4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6}{4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\,024}{24} \\\\  & =126.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67455965-c9d3-5ae8-8b56-2a58af95df0a" :knowledge-point/title "Stating the Vertex of a Vertical Parabola Given Its Equation in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "454a079a-3b5d-5568-a29d-6f2901c3f216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352210} {:answer/id #uuid "66130b54-d958-5449-b32e-e09731f751e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352466} {:answer/id #uuid "72d2ceff-58e4-5cc6-ae01-b9e7dcc9c275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352571} {:answer/id #uuid "75807951-bdad-56c4-9ac3-85b96c3b3874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352599} {:answer/id #uuid "bf82ba33-b385-51d9-aff0-7b68257c69a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75807951-bdad-56c4-9ac3-85b96c3b3874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352599} :answer-field/id #uuid "46991009-d0a6-523f-9684-9c96c88c491a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2eb1bd6-8295-533c-aa8b-65d0d7da5480" :question/math-academy-id "q-84967" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(x+3)^{2}=5y?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{5}{4})(y-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=-3$ and $k=0.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(-3,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354375 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096763 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e302810-8b14-5c8c-8bbb-245386a66b7c" :knowledge-point/title "Stating the Vertex of a Horizontal Parabola Given Its Equation in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30951a52-0069-56b4-9e82-2949d8f2a2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352014} {:answer/id #uuid "398fdaaf-9bcb-5cc4-a059-9e1bd02851bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352098} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad09f62-dd43-5478-8c92-4ff635da99d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352255} {:answer/id #uuid "a86861b4-432a-5da2-8768-af22fdb06c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352997} {:answer/id #uuid "daf7b837-7463-559a-9f9e-1ebc8d42ec4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a86861b4-432a-5da2-8768-af22fdb06c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352997} :answer-field/id #uuid "9374288c-3b96-5e55-b494-e802520d3be4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a083623-223f-5e1c-9de8-75212036d214" :question/math-academy-id "q-84975" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${y}^{2}=-8x?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4(-2)x.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-2.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "060e80f0-44bd-5378-9253-999548115dd9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Permutations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "290f943b-3622-50d0-924f-31daf0acc563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351948} {:answer/id #uuid "77ef416e-a3b0-5473-bf35-8e1898c31847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352617} {:answer/id #uuid "7b5e835c-ce12-5b70-842e-5afce9455637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352642} {:answer/id #uuid "82456399-e756-555d-81e7-d2b347aafff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352701} {:answer/id #uuid "f32745fa-711d-5685-96d5-140a1218db12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82456399-e756-555d-81e7-d2b347aafff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352701} :answer-field/id #uuid "07f7e8b6-11ec-5218-9222-810bc50fc8fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fda58513-e80c-5984-860c-d5cee3ac12ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-84980" :question/problem "How many made-up $3$-letter words can be made using only $5$ vowels, where each vowel cannot appear in the word more than once?" :question/worked-solution "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $5$ vowels, so $n=5.$ We want to select $3$ of those vowels to make-up a $3$-letter word, so we have $r=3.$ \n\nTherefore, the number of different made-up words is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{5}{P}_{3} & =\\frac{5!}{(5-3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot \\cancel{2!}}{\\cancel{2!}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "060e80f0-44bd-5378-9253-999548115dd9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Permutations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaed84db-333e-5211-84c5-2d24c1d90316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,016" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394622} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c1b16c-2ec4-5950-92f8-59f867978e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394623} {:answer/id #uuid "542940cc-ac17-5702-9c22-d185b7bb47c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394624} {:answer/id #uuid "2e70f827-6004-57e0-825e-f0e45fc164d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,344" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394625} {:answer/id #uuid "3d0a0e84-7492-5601-b7a4-02b8e70ea90d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1c1b16c-2ec4-5950-92f8-59f867978e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394623} :answer-field/id #uuid "72d9fa78-c6ea-5ff0-a6cb-0b7ec2cbcfc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ef67e9d-56e9-502f-9f38-dd41f4d01e7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-84983" :question/problem "A bag contains $9$ different colored marbles. Jamie takes $4$ marbles from the bag, one at a time, and puts them in the order they were taken from the bag to create a sequence of colors. How many different sequences of colors are possible?" :question/worked-solution "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $9$ colored marbles, so $n=9.$ We want to select $4$ of those marbles to make a sequence of colors, so we have $r=4.$ \n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to choose the marbles is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{9}{P}_{4} & =\\frac{9!}{(9-4)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9!}{5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{\\cancel{5!}} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =3\\,024.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6b720d4-59b0-5d86-b856-ff69abfe4152" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Combinations Given Using Alternate Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43fa39e5-ce50-500d-851f-de9eb3b12eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19\\,380" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394629} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e55eef-a2a6-5dcc-8dbd-442ffb6f3b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,480" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394630} {:answer/id #uuid "24b6f013-8ec7-5811-963d-26ce3311756c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,860" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394631} {:answer/id #uuid "cde0fe8a-36a8-5e30-8de3-aa6250e1a52c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,280" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394632} {:answer/id #uuid "f9dca18c-0cda-53f2-ae56-98a74295c88b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,845" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9dca18c-0cda-53f2-ae56-98a74295c88b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,845" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394633} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee128c64-8462-52f2-aa05-6a02c9deb300" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ccb665c6-b272-5591-95cb-e38be54fe76e" :question/math-academy-id "q-85044" :question/problem "Evaluate $(\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $(\\frac{20}{16})$ means the same thing as ${}_{20}{C}_{16}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=20$ and $r=16,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{20}{C}_{16} & =\\frac{20!}{(20-16)!16!} \\\\  & =\\frac{20!}{4!16!} \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\cdot 19\\cdot 18\\cdot 17\\cdot \\cancel{16!}}{4!\\cancel{16!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\cdot 19\\cdot 18\\cdot 17}{4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{116\\,280}{24} \\\\  & =4\\,845.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9c145aa-bcbf-54bd-ba88-93f10aeb00f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Permutation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcef49e9-f6e2-5903-88c5-5668ea726fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,652" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394636} {:answer/id #uuid "00becd7c-07c7-51a1-8d01-c22ee90258d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,326" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394637} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbc2ffb-e921-5ab1-a79b-de22cae70506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,248" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394638} {:answer/id #uuid "f4f8eb3e-2322-5192-b908-a3996052fac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,304" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394639} {:answer/id #uuid "043e4ba5-d6b6-52c1-acd4-3439e275df92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcef49e9-f6e2-5903-88c5-5668ea726fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,652" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394636} :answer-field/id #uuid "66c1eba9-fa9c-5dcd-af76-d9dc0545ee45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6727864-63ba-5449-a9b7-0dc7ad66d6bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-85046" :question/problem "${}_{52}{P}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for ${}_{n}{P}_{r}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=52$ and $r=2$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{52}{P}_{2} & =\\frac{52!}{(52-2)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{52!}{50!} \\\\  & =\\frac{52\\cdot 51\\cdot \\cancel{50!}}{\\cancel{50!}} \\\\  & =52\\cdot 51 \\\\  & =2\\,652.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7c35604-c9f9-5077-bddc-fe9446ac45e6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Permutation Given Using Alternate Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9972d5bf-226c-5e87-988b-2fa3e0b4fbd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394643} {:answer/id #uuid "82c57328-7703-57d6-912b-2ecb7333260d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394644} {:answer/id #uuid "57d5dc3c-cc5e-57df-b81a-64cfa2b4c5cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394645} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc1825b-3805-54c0-b2fd-b20b91c26d42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394646} {:answer/id #uuid "4c2571b5-b785-5912-b9bf-d0e4a3fa1a43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82c57328-7703-57d6-912b-2ecb7333260d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394644} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff3b3c1d-8754-5e1f-a814-29025fd4b1b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1198cf2-96b8-5b7c-bdc4-b79e3231eb29" :question/math-academy-id "q-85049" :question/problem "$P(7,7)=$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for ${}_{n}{P}_{r}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}\\,.\n$$\n\n$P(7,7)$ means the same thing as ${}_{7}{P}_{7}.$ Using the usual formula for ${}_{n}{P}_{r},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(7,7) & =\\frac{7!}{(7-7)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7!}{0!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}{1} \\\\  & =5\\,040.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404315 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129082 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06cba355-0ee9-5493-b5b4-b01907ba7853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404317} {:answer/id #uuid "88bc01f1-e620-5d5f-b135-3c829509a914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404318} {:answer/id #uuid "c44c5852-2bff-553c-b41d-6c60dda93e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404319} {:answer/id #uuid "e6647b56-89a1-5a81-85bf-14ccfa47147b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404320} {:answer/id #uuid "814df066-a51f-5318-8a89-ed554769eecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6647b56-89a1-5a81-85bf-14ccfa47147b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404320} :answer-field/id #uuid "9151fede-a06c-511f-acdd-16c15e3d7d4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37e87bc7-80c8-5654-8c99-e96b9d9f6c6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-85640" :question/problem "![](images/97/97c98411b3455c78c9e061cf54fa76eabfccc369e9e364a5366eb58bd5d2935d.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{7},$ what is the value of $cot⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/8c/8c7889d63cf5894236bc5f5e24feb64ecf5a47acb2c07c807a4e65458a991232.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{45}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is negative in the fourth quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $cot⁡\\theta =\\frac{cos⁡\\theta }{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{7})}{(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})}=-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404322 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a09362-632a-56c4-b246-903e18fc77ee" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bce3c75-6a0d-53d7-9a0d-6b6d8acd31bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404324} {:answer/id #uuid "2613efdb-cd4c-5ddd-8173-e348bdcb96f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404325} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7b0ef2-5302-5b29-89fc-f8f26371b54c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404326} {:answer/id #uuid "39dd9d4a-1c32-59d2-b731-ba230d302e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404327} {:answer/id #uuid "b99f4a1b-d16f-5aa6-a399-a33703bd010f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bce3c75-6a0d-53d7-9a0d-6b6d8acd31bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404324} :answer-field/id #uuid "e48430d0-09a9-535a-843b-f4fdfbee498f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0902cd66-9d18-50eb-a894-3b1edfc4b17b" :question/math-academy-id "q-85643" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3be6fa94deb7fc38d870e5a31cc81c3cfd6b9ab84c21340e7e88f7246b1058bc.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{5}{7},$ what is the value of $sec⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/17/17e6320115f2cf8b5ffad6abc099169774536af20d2559b453aaa97c1286d96d.png)\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{5}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{7}.$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{5}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{24}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{24}}{7} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})}=-\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{6}}=-\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{12}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28f4d12d-55f3-5551-a993-70590cc67cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "278df47a-986d-53c8-9bf8-7218a7775d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351929} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4a255b-0aef-5b5e-9440-4dcc51125122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352653} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ca7fb9-c250-5183-956b-1632f25a63c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353147} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ea0ef5-4ad0-5921-abb6-ec4af7740b0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353552} {:answer/id #uuid "ec524a21-355f-5fa1-9900-49c8c930bba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c4a255b-0aef-5b5e-9440-4dcc51125122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352653} :answer-field/id #uuid "02fb8878-5fec-5527-ba2f-c326bfe63142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea81e60a-b3ce-5060-848f-f21e99caa4c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-8577" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{6}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{6})=-0.167\\,448…≈-0.167\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-0.167$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-0.167\\,4.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.167\\,4.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.167\\,4.$\n\n![](images/f6/f6f08d799cd2d2e80494d94cc3b18a61f73227b88b25d79c926d65e161d4ce3a.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.167\\,4 \\\\  & ≈3.309\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=X-2\\pi  & ≈3.309-2\\pi ≈-2.974\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-0.167\\,4)\\cdot (-2.974\\,2)≈0.5,\n$$\n\nrounded to $1$ decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "236cec41-4836-5526-893d-e6f2a23d1801" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12788cbb-a78d-5eff-ac3d-7309a6c971b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{355}{1+{e}^{-t}}$ liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351725} {:answer/id #uuid "1bf2fe45-b3b9-5c97-b1bb-c7df2b59a6ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{710{e}^{t}}{2+{e}^{-t}}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351818} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9337cc-7a9c-50b8-8966-c08de1912686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{710}{1+{e}^{-t}}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351975} {:answer/id #uuid "35aeabef-1c3d-5a27-b9ce-17d6c7474cba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$710{e}^{-t}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352067} {:answer/id #uuid "62e25f9f-c9c4-5c73-8e53-f310197acc34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$355{e}^{t}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c9337cc-7a9c-50b8-8966-c08de1912686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{710}{1+{e}^{-t}}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351975} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f24ed36-8d2c-53fe-81db-4d1f78c00dca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3efcfb8-9934-5ac8-a968-8c9e18c90fc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-86191" :question/problem "The function $P(t)=710\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{e}^{t})$ represents the volume of milk, in liters, produced on a farm $t$ days after the owner bought new cows. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the volume of produced milk as a function of $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the volume of produced milk with respect to time is ${P}^{′}(t).$\n\nNow, the rate of change of $P(t)$ with respect to $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(710\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{e}^{t})) \\\\  & =710\\,\\frac{{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{710}{1+{e}^{-t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the volume of produced milk is  $\\frac{710}{1+{e}^{-t}}$ liters per day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0052499-42ab-5818-a844-20eb099c3f7b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sine or Cosine of a Special Angle Given in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "044b0751-3888-55cc-a3f5-80549082bb96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351606} {:answer/id #uuid "7b30bf64-0acc-5282-b803-06945c32a50f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352639} {:answer/id #uuid "92bdbad7-2d48-5c77-876c-fcc57313f9ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352838} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0f11de-1be9-5064-990b-af0dfef7ce48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352930} {:answer/id #uuid "dec91441-cb91-59c4-a043-47f7c0f1aa32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b30bf64-0acc-5282-b803-06945c32a50f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352639} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c5e282a-f0f5-5ba8-8e6f-9a5d8748314c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb464a1e-3e09-57c1-9d5b-ec060c590e24" :question/math-academy-id "q-86327" :question/problem "$cos⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $cos⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15c0277c-fb4b-5026-9fd8-6c7810e057b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351756} {:answer/id #uuid "1e6dd320-c836-51a4-ad23-8614799db41b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351838} {:answer/id #uuid "5f17f867-8ede-528f-b531-5e234c5f7333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352410} {:answer/id #uuid "67375961-39e8-54d5-9054-515f282d6534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352482} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a357ac-56ed-5863-b3dc-01fa6b42eb7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3a357ac-56ed-5863-b3dc-01fa6b42eb7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353259} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d77c46d-1aa3-501f-aae2-671960790f6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "682dd404-2e33-5194-9b75-83eececc2df8" :question/math-academy-id "q-86350" :question/problem "The following table shows the velocity of a plane during a $20$-minute trip.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{minutes})$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)\\,(\\text{kilometers/minute})$ | $17$ | $25$ | $27$ | $k$ | $18$ |\n\nIf the right Riemann sum approximation of the total distance traveled during this period has a value of  $450\\,\\text{km}$, what is the value of $k?$\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the velocity-time graph.*" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the curve $y=v(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{v(5)+v(10)+v(15)+v(20)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $v(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)$ | $25$ | $27$ | $k$ | $18$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(25+27+k+18)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5(k+70) \\\\  & =5k+350\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total distance traveled during the $20$ minute period. Since we are given that the right Riemann sum approximation of the total distance is $450\\,\\text{km},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5k+350 & =450 \\\\ 5k & =450-350 \\\\ k & =\\frac{100}{5} \\\\ k & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7f04ab7-f6f6-5d01-bfcf-b667e37e4f0b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation When the Solutions Are Irrational"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "029fa0e3-dd36-5f3b-83ad-2cba2e31a6df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351588} {:answer/id #uuid "0347f6ba-8275-55a4-aac2-653e481c8564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351595} {:answer/id #uuid "371907b8-8d3b-547a-9416-21dcd94a96bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352079} {:answer/id #uuid "64af757f-a5f5-5de8-9518-714b14936255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352457} {:answer/id #uuid "67a3e342-6a19-5ca4-9745-f0c27bfd5f69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0347f6ba-8275-55a4-aac2-653e481c8564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351595} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b5c2324-629b-5b3c-99c4-45b9f2afb50e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6913ef2e-5530-525b-8ba5-681a643ef5b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-86404" :question/problem "The equation ${y}^{2}+16=-12y$ can be written in the form $(y+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, the $-12y$ term to the left-hand side, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+16 & =-12y \\\\ {y}^{2}+12y & =-16 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot 6\\cdot y & =-16 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot 6\\cdot y+{6}^{2}-{6}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ (y+6)^{2}-{6}^{2} & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+6)^{2}-{6}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ (y+6)^{2} & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=6,q=20,$ and $p+q=26.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b68c25b-c193-5a40-829b-fd51b04788c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404331} {:answer/id #uuid "0cd1cdc0-3122-5e73-85f9-af559fd24792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=2,\\,w=\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404332} {:answer/id #uuid "7c200ff8-dd75-5421-837d-9ef23ff5522e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=2,\\,w=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404333} {:answer/id #uuid "5671ea71-84dd-554c-92ae-a5caaefc57b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=-2\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404334} {:answer/id #uuid "34743567-dc8f-5027-8a31-2da4c77b759c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34743567-dc8f-5027-8a31-2da4c77b759c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404335} :answer-field/id #uuid "3733f599-455f-5b42-9817-59fac3c5944c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7895edb1-f2c2-5302-ad9e-dc0b6ea9c033" :question/math-academy-id "q-86413" :question/problem "The equation $-4{w}^{2}-4w+11=0$ can be written in the form $4(w+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{w}^{2}-4w+11) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}+4w-11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{2}+4w-11 & =0 \\\\ 4({w}^{2}+w) & =11 \\\\ 4[{w}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}w+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}] & =11 \\\\ 4[{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} & =11 \\\\ 4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-1 & =11 \\\\ 4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =11+1 \\\\ 4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=12.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{12}{4} \\\\ {(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ |w+\\frac{1}{2}| & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ w+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ w & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c07dfad2-047a-527a-a51a-4f998f1949f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404345} {:answer/id #uuid "fff341dd-b41a-5304-ad09-cc4af765adfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=15,\\,w=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404346} {:answer/id #uuid "0b80197a-b970-5c44-9c91-75cf359e8575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=5$ and $w=\\frac{11}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404347} {:answer/id #uuid "fe959228-e459-5b19-9cf7-7d8a7111d3eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=15,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404348} {:answer/id #uuid "89145d06-76ec-5ca5-93f9-df5b33533d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$ and $w=15$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c07dfad2-047a-527a-a51a-4f998f1949f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404345} :answer-field/id #uuid "417df0a0-d7e8-5502-826d-e0777e84a95f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "211d779e-92bf-5786-beb2-56b9bec0ebf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-86415" :question/problem "The equation $-4{w}^{2}+40w-75=0$ can be written in the form $4(w+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{w}^{2}+40w-75) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}-40w+75 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{2}-40w+75 & =0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}-40w & =-75 \\\\ 4({w}^{2}-10w) & =-75 \\\\ 4[{w}^{2}-2\\cdot 5w+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2}] & =-75 \\\\ 4[{(w-5)}^{2}-25] & =-75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w-5)}^{2}-4\\cdot 25 & =-75 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2}-100 & =-75 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2} & =-75+100 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=25.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w-5)}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {(w-5)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(w-5)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |w-5| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w-5 & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w & =5\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =5+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{15}{2} \\\\ w & =5-\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a227c7a8-977d-54b0-9409-d364da03656f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Mean of a Data Set From the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30c20a01-270f-5737-ae9a-84d63a1f7fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352015} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3569a6-6912-57e6-a85c-9238f40ddbba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352272} {:answer/id #uuid "afdfff25-ae14-5a9e-93f1-0dc0a031e75e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353070} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e84888-7272-5abb-8f63-afaab6047bda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353419} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0af2fb-0e67-5b16-95b0-3d374048e7ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30c20a01-270f-5737-ae9a-84d63a1f7fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352015} :answer-field/id #uuid "af5b7a45-08a6-51e0-b39b-62df1bc74ed8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b510547-4334-5503-aa02-8d3585bbd83f" :question/math-academy-id "q-86566" :question/problem "Given a data set with $15$ data points and ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{x}_{i}=91,$ compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=15$ and ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{x}_{i}=91,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{15}\\cdot 91≈6.1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have rounded our answer to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d1ea5c4-2700-5c03-a22e-de5439f47c36" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Quotient of Complex Numbers in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60fd086a-cc61-541f-9b4b-ec2cc540caf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352430} {:answer/id #uuid "7a2bb3a1-bc8f-52e8-a927-560042cb773c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352629} {:answer/id #uuid "8a31dec4-0251-5466-8e47-232c23d35fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352771} {:answer/id #uuid "eaade135-d169-51ea-9c83-f963ae7caf70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353614} {:answer/id #uuid "f84688b7-621e-5077-87eb-67f12a891b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60fd086a-cc61-541f-9b4b-ec2cc540caf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352430} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc216d6-cfc8-5143-86bf-f3205a392eb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99c9ca4c-ed0c-591e-b38c-417492420a68" :question/math-academy-id "q-86605" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{2}{1+\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $1-\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{2}{1+\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\text{i})}{(1+\\text{i})(1-\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\text{i})}{{1}^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\text{i})}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\text{i})}{2} \\\\  & =1-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=1-\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=1$ is positive and $y=-1$ is negative, $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-1}{1}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1 \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}))\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d1ea5c4-2700-5c03-a22e-de5439f47c36" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Quotient of Complex Numbers in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46b3b120-4bfd-561a-a0e7-62cf46b09991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4.234)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4.234)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352221} {:answer/id #uuid "4dab2719-a4ec-51ca-bfdd-fe5386738a02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.640)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.640)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352277} {:answer/id #uuid "94ba7390-71ef-5b2d-9d57-336b9260fcde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3.348)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3.348)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352857} {:answer/id #uuid "ba94df56-ced2-592c-8dad-aa00a5d37c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4.234)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4.234))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353154} {:answer/id #uuid "f2055884-7713-5a15-ab40-90c79f656a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.640)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.640))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dab2719-a4ec-51ca-bfdd-fe5386738a02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.640)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.640)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352277} :answer-field/id #uuid "18c1098f-ad3c-5446-b0ff-b35acf26d420" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "350143d0-75f7-5bce-a045-8667ae979c3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-86606" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{5}{4+3\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give $\\theta $ in radians to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $4-3\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{5}{4+3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(4-3\\text{i})}{(4+3\\text{i})(4-3\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(4-3\\text{i})}{{4}^{2}-9{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(4-3\\text{i})}{16+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(4-3\\text{i})}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}+\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16+9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $ represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{4}{5}$ is positive and $y=-\\frac{3}{5}$ is negative, $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{5})}{(\\frac{4}{5})}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & ≈5.640,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.640)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.640)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092352 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49bf1f94-d992-511d-9016-d471f2ad4358" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates for a Point on an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f66708d-1547-50f2-92c0-56d1bde53f01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351842} {:answer/id #uuid "56f2b546-03d9-5653-9d2e-bf0088f32423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352334} {:answer/id #uuid "82b58078-9c1d-529e-9dd7-3d4c5bbb9486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352707} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7dc7a6-d51e-5412-9d8a-79341eb1ef22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352923} {:answer/id #uuid "af6e4719-59bb-50dd-accf-820318d61365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56f2b546-03d9-5653-9d2e-bf0088f32423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352334} :answer-field/id #uuid "b19692bb-7833-5ae5-8617-ee2a519e7c8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d112c03c-5e89-5df5-a3c4-c5b7e8bba175" :question/math-academy-id "q-86616" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(4,0).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the positive half of the $x$-axis, we have $\\theta =0.$ \n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(4,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e49e2595-62a1-595a-8df9-99541bccbdf5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Permutations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "087937ab-4446-5368-9bce-1fce43b01fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "480" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351643} {:answer/id #uuid "62d604ea-fe2e-5a98-826f-4071e1838830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "910" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352445} {:answer/id #uuid "99c0e736-b604-56b8-b963-6ab73e357502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352885} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd10b1b-d951-577b-8d78-314446269a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "540" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353161} {:answer/id #uuid "faf4206d-50cc-5507-9c11-16e00ad05a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99c0e736-b604-56b8-b963-6ab73e357502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352885} :answer-field/id #uuid "65e3c440-dfb0-5d0a-a444-7331a8e9d7d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1af97a6c-2901-5306-ad12-401b993aaea1" :question/math-academy-id "q-86869" :question/problem "A railway train has $6$ passenger wagons. How many ways can $6$ conductors be distributed among the wagons if one conductor is assigned to each wagon?" :question/worked-solution "There are $6$  conductors to arrange, so there are $6!$ ways of arranging them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6! & =6\\times 5\\times \\times 3\\times 2\\times 1 \\\\  & =720.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a227c7a8-977d-54b0-9409-d364da03656f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Mean of a Data Set From the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19d54d1f-efb8-552f-ab3b-abbbe3b0f8a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351794} {:answer/id #uuid "2e2993ee-9bdd-5531-bd61-349cf661922e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351991} {:answer/id #uuid "56cb2b30-74c2-59e2-8165-0d814424a123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352333} {:answer/id #uuid "873b25b6-3d91-5f71-aa9e-c71a735899af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352751} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e9a2e7-cbd5-5bb1-942a-2f9b29401f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56cb2b30-74c2-59e2-8165-0d814424a123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352333} :answer-field/id #uuid "521666e1-0f9d-5046-b997-80486f7e0f9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81127073-3815-575d-a148-cb1ef2bb975d" :question/math-academy-id "q-86932" :question/problem "Given a data set with $23$ data points and ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{23}{x}_{i}=590,$ compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=23$ and ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{23}{x}_{i}=590,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{23}\\cdot 590≈25.7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have rounded our answer to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93f33baf-c3b5-549a-8eb7-1ab370e9c62b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Polar Circle With an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e80d3a5-235b-5aa9-abf3-3b8a292ec2fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351689} {:answer/id #uuid "23deca39-4e9a-5e7d-83b6-132ff134e0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3.5,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351893} {:answer/id #uuid "255a7dae-b2c9-53ff-a8d5-406d95919015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351908} {:answer/id #uuid "c8b498f0-f1fa-5727-93bd-6fe6823c485b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3.5,\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353315} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb889a3-fe15-51e9-843e-0fc93821ca84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3.5,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23deca39-4e9a-5e7d-83b6-132ff134e0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3.5,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351893} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c97de37-dc6d-5b1e-9e01-8c441bb816ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ab7847b-1891-54e9-9c41-f389a5063166" :question/math-academy-id "q-86947" :question/problem "Find the **polar** coordinates of the point where the polar curve $r=3.5$ intersects the positive $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "The polar curve $r=3.5$ is a circle of radius $3.5$ centered at the origin. It crosses the positive $y$-axis at the point where $r=3.5$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/dc/dc12f535f40d75a21fc367bf6e08cde9f4ec086c4f358e4ab198a6f44da1aac4.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(3.5,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6b41a05-7b11-52b8-a407-af505dffaf10" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Data Set Using the Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b345208-118e-5652-b22b-098c78c034f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351665} {:answer/id #uuid "43267825-1da1-549f-b40f-13640900b0c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352185} {:answer/id #uuid "756d7bb4-ec1d-5b8d-9ec1-bdd7581a5796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352597} {:answer/id #uuid "a95b216b-5707-5c1c-9ebf-22afc3b7925d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{37}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353003} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e627b0-c925-5cfa-9749-80dbb8d824eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a95b216b-5707-5c1c-9ebf-22afc3b7925d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{37}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353003} :answer-field/id #uuid "725eaeaf-88ac-5d74-9ca0-28e3a580f020" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a33c9758-e856-52e3-9ca0-8b4d2b8f793f" :question/math-academy-id "q-87464" :question/problem "What is the variance of the following data set?\n\n$$\n-3,\\,1,\\,-5,\\,-5,\\,2,\\,-8\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ is the mean of the data set.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the mean. There are $n=6$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-3+1-5-5+2-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-18) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[(-3-(-3))^{2}+(1-(-3))^{2}+(-5-(-3))^{2}+(-5-(-3))^{2}+(2-(-3))^{2}+(-8-(-3))^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[0+16+4+4+25+25] \\\\  & =\\frac{74}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{37}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115363 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0040fc1-2134-5eaa-a3e8-6c7a457f75f5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Data Set Using the Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d194127-5565-5ea6-9149-fb729c34c731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351826} {:answer/id #uuid "249d55e5-0b47-5cef-9a5d-7c2c9ce22b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351903} {:answer/id #uuid "60dc0e33-8bff-54f1-9044-88c8aa366515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352429} {:answer/id #uuid "618c0075-6c89-5ce7-8205-c22c83cbce81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352439} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c1e82b-4a36-567b-b964-7eae626ffb2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d194127-5565-5ea6-9149-fb729c34c731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351826} :answer-field/id #uuid "e885e1fb-3ce2-57f1-87d2-e78acd0124d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e1e3be6-fb14-565f-8535-3adfd3c2eec9" :question/math-academy-id "q-87606" :question/problem "What is the variance of the following data set?\n\n$$\n1,\\,5,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,6,\\,1,\\,2,\\,6\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=9$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (1+5+1+2+3+6+1+2+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot ({1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (1+25+1+4+9+36+1+4+36) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 117 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =13-{(3)}^{2} \\\\  & =13-9 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115364 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a72dae6-2bbf-5e59-b71d-8ee57f589753" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Standard Deviation of a Data Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "427e4e36-8e8f-518b-8674-8478d60112ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{34}{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352177} {:answer/id #uuid "b0adc768-befd-5424-a027-a014f92b2b70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{31}{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353078} {:answer/id #uuid "d7ae299a-102c-532a-a8e9-1db85591ce85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{97}{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353439} {:answer/id #uuid "f38cadee-21b7-58cf-9f69-13b52ed1e1a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{55}{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353697} {:answer/id #uuid "f4963773-d74e-538c-9779-c3ce7fcedcde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{87}{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "427e4e36-8e8f-518b-8674-8478d60112ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{34}{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352177} :answer-field/id #uuid "a48d3bea-1363-5f06-866b-d0c35b8f69dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c5f5a511-45ef-5fe1-b914-88ccd3d31b71" :question/math-academy-id "q-87612" :question/problem "What is the standard deviation of the following data set?\n\n$$\n7,\\,3,\\,3,\\,5,\\,1,\\,0,\\,2\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The standard deviation of a data set ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is the square root of the variance:\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\,\\,\\,\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=7$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (7+3+3+5+1+0+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 21 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot ({7}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (49+9+9+25+1+0+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{97}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{97}{7}-{(3)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{97}{7}-9 \\\\  & =\\frac{97-63}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{34}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the standard deviation is\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{34}{7}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115364 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a72dae6-2bbf-5e59-b71d-8ee57f589753" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Standard Deviation of a Data Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1715cab9-06ff-5775-8221-04bf4db52149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{44}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351771} {:answer/id #uuid "2286aa66-4634-5f2a-86f3-a4cdcce1fc98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{124}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351878} {:answer/id #uuid "44f1270a-9110-58f6-8fa9-8ef77b30ed2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{48}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352202} {:answer/id #uuid "56928ff8-cc5a-56f5-92ac-4b2a6100b8b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{41}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352330} {:answer/id #uuid "ddb6a08a-013d-586d-a88a-e2d2b3e0595d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{37}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1715cab9-06ff-5775-8221-04bf4db52149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{44}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351771} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7a93a33-8e48-5ada-8662-5f5d2efbde64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab0de973-6daf-5f45-9069-960a74229aa4" :question/math-academy-id "q-87613" :question/problem "What is the standard deviation of the following data set?\n\n$$\n1,\\,1,\\,5,\\,4,\\,9\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The standard deviation of a data set ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is the square root of the variance:\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\,\\,\\,\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=5$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (1+1+5+4+9) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot ({1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{9}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (1+1+25+16+81) \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{5}-{(4)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{5}-16 \\\\  & =\\frac{124-80}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{44}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the standard deviation is\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{44}{5}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6445834c-7788-5427-a72c-30b06cc8e6ab" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of an Isosceles Right Triangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0663bea7-12f5-5928-84d7-bc207c70bfaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351623} {:answer/id #uuid "3101c5fb-53ba-5942-ad43-1ae35079f738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352018} {:answer/id #uuid "7e914068-1f39-55f3-a988-748244647a92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352677} {:answer/id #uuid "8150fcea-b946-540d-b0fd-6ae56c257637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352692} {:answer/id #uuid "aa336a88-ddef-5077-bcae-4e7bf818c424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3101c5fb-53ba-5942-ad43-1ae35079f738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352018} :answer-field/id #uuid "69982ee7-0741-5c08-835f-b26951ee678f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "298d5629-725b-5aa5-9d76-4ed61398d66b" :question/math-academy-id "q-87802" :question/problem "![](images/37/3756b00cce29aad77b7a0b1ebb09f43cb89a602f8462edce89e8ba6346cb7fab.png)\n\nWhat is the area of the right triangle $ABC$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Since $m∠A=m∠B$, we have an isosceles triangle, where $AC=BC.$\n\nHence, the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {10}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 100 \\\\  & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7656d23-06c9-5d22-b62a-3636bca66ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Base or the Height of a Triangle Given Its Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ef35ba9-a1a6-5901-a68b-21603e2051d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404428} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf3609b-ef5e-5b0b-9c1a-f2b156f94734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404429} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f39be9-b1e0-5587-868d-9f77d0afc794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404430} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9dd512-8f07-5391-be34-d4707734b6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404431} {:answer/id #uuid "7c69f1fa-94c7-5dbc-8f48-c65d49727acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdf3609b-ef5e-5b0b-9c1a-f2b156f94734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404429} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d266bcc-fa01-524b-89f8-39a6f9df7aea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7bcc27a5-b987-5302-94f2-988d56293740" :question/math-academy-id "q-87910" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c19425664f79df1f46863f59268ebd27e20defce41b21e877926bbcadc9e5c3.png)\n\nConsider the triangles $△ABC$ and $△CDE$ shown above. If the area of $△CDE$ is $42{\\text{ cm}}^{2}$, find the area of $△ABC.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider $△CDE.$ We can see that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is the height corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{DE}$ and that $DE=7\\text{ cm}.$ \n\nSince the area of $△CDE$ is $42{\\text{ cm}}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 42 & =\\frac{7\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\ 7CD & =84 \\\\ CD & =12\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is also the height of $△ABC$ corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n![](images/00/0014faf05eaf15d70f885802e5b820b0c72d42a96f400442f827ed229de8b240.png)\n\nTherefore, we find the area of $△ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{AB\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 12}{2} \\\\  & =30{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087660 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6f0c89e-fe19-5aaf-9f85-b5f0ff7a344e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27d78e8d-440e-5a7d-9de7-6040a2af40bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351933} {:answer/id #uuid "5095bfe7-409f-5c40-bafa-1d687fa34ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352292} {:answer/id #uuid "ad987978-69c9-55f1-91dc-45c328ea9c6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353037} {:answer/id #uuid "c56a25ac-0fb1-5716-8be6-e0135ef5b387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353275} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9afad1-88a0-5806-bc5e-c902f6cabec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad987978-69c9-55f1-91dc-45c328ea9c6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353037} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa897115-6a9c-5563-a32e-dff389f39adc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "591b47fb-ebcd-5f48-9b95-6d9ef3025f87" :question/math-academy-id "q-8794" :question/problem "Calculate the $y$-intercept of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x-2y+1=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We must have $x=0$ at the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ {0}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4\\cdot 0-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-1 & =0 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y$-intercept occurs at the point $(0,1),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/87/87766af5840b6b3729fa200d383e18c5f7926d9000633f99b481e8b4a6922732.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087659 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9faf6d8-0c06-5bf4-bc41-6d68d3d414c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f34793f4-d1bc-55ef-ae53-5c1f899e1dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle has no $x$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087306} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf2c342-a2a1-504b-9f09-bce2765f6388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4, 0)$ and $(-2, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087307} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb476fe-3ff9-5dd8-a59a-816f32f1cc85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087308} {:answer/id #uuid "4e729635-4669-5cc0-a772-4b13036834c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087309} {:answer/id #uuid "526883e8-3397-542b-8212-e2dc7e759528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4, 0)$ and $(2, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087310}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b85c01aa-1d3f-537a-9d1a-63a72a7d7810" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087305} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ef4cfa7-a528-5a44-81bf-1eb05ec48919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,0)$ and $(-2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396218} {:answer/id #uuid "3334e26d-26a5-5a8d-86e1-8735e21e1c02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4,0)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396219} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd0c780-9ea5-5b64-9aa4-a2787dad3113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle has no $x$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396220} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e0824b-8114-51cb-acce-f982515426bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396221} {:answer/id #uuid "4a918d4f-45e7-5d0c-8cd6-0ec507b2d1fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bd0c780-9ea5-5b64-9aa4-a2787dad3113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle has no $x$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396220} :answer-field/id #uuid "c01f4ef8-bdc2-5c0b-ae80-d7c2da907244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb231c9b-8ce3-5939-ba7a-76e32364f988" :question/math-academy-id "q-8801" :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=3$" :question/worked-solution "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(0-2)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+4 & =3 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x+3)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x,$ there is no $x$ such that $(x+3)^{2}=-1.$ Therefore, circle has no $x$-intercepts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cb/cb8a943a6f6cfd49e47c193f5379e06fd166cd17087c890233b42a7e1e7d2b89.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef56dae1-235e-58a4-9d49-dac58019c11c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394781} {:answer/id #uuid "5d542604-cc75-5fe8-a314-152f5e0d0baf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394782} {:answer/id #uuid "98750137-8ecd-58a9-830f-28a32bdedfa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394783} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e12eec-8e62-581b-b20f-14c8b66d8602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394784} {:answer/id #uuid "57fa678f-41a4-5b0a-9cfb-0034cbdf2533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57fa678f-41a4-5b0a-9cfb-0034cbdf2533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394785} :answer-field/id #uuid "e16451bc-a0a9-548c-ba07-f9abe1416b11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3d7afbe-c052-55bc-910e-6663c48abfcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-88459" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+12x\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+10\\cdot {2}^{x-1}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+12x\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+10\\cdot {2}^{x-1} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+12x+10){2}^{x-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+12x+10=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{2}^{x-1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}+12x+10=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n2(x+1)(x+5)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-5,-1.$\n\n- The equation ${2}^{x-1}=0$ has no solutions because ${2}^{x-1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-5,-1,$ and their product is $(-5)\\cdot (-1)=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07eb336-1ac8-5633-abaf-fcfca4c12bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Intersections"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ca39c86-c237-57c2-8d8c-dda21442afec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355347} {:answer/id #uuid "8ce90c57-4877-5774-9681-0f9aa958e585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355909} {:answer/id #uuid "9280f538-12fe-5a96-8f3c-4ff956dd093f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355957} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb85bb0-6fa4-59b2-ad41-5c5cd393e251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356060} {:answer/id #uuid "d5bb5ddb-c925-56df-85f5-227622e09a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ca39c86-c237-57c2-8d8c-dda21442afec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355347} :answer-field/id #uuid "26ad4582-1eee-5ebd-8785-6a3cf5b49efc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db570458-831d-5df7-87a7-8b916c4f12ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-88918" :question/problem "A pet center registered $34$ dogs and $29$ cats on a particular day. Of the dogs registered that day, $12$ were vaccinated. Of the cats registered that day, $15$ were vaccinated. What is the probability that a randomly selected pet registered that day was vaccinated?" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the event that the chosen pet was vaccinated, and let $D$ be the event that the pet is a dog. Then ${D}^{′}$ is the event that the pet is a cat.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected pet was vaccinated, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(V)=P(V\\cap D)+P(V\\cap {D}^{′})\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(V\\cap D),$ we take the number of dogs who visited the pet center and were vaccinated $(12)$ and divide by the total number of pets $(34+29=63).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(V\\cap D)=\\frac{12}{63}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(V\\cap {D}^{′}),$ we take the number of cats who visited the pet center and were vaccinated $(15)$ and divide by the total number of pets $(63).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(V\\cap {D}^{′})=\\frac{15}{63}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected pet was vaccinated is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(V) & =P(V\\cap D)+P(V\\cap {D}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{63}+\\frac{15}{63} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{63} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0f7055d-9f45-5115-97f4-35d0c56008e0" :knowledge-point/title "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "177abed1-3340-52f4-b1b1-3e9d3c91a06c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354875} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce948d9-38e5-5268-a841-288391c7dc4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356063} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d2bdc3-4057-5d74-97aa-8279dd26fa33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356091} {:answer/id #uuid "b51115cb-95b0-5606-8be1-1cd205702798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356257} {:answer/id #uuid "c42cb318-2ebc-50d6-8303-ec19ea35ed9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0d2bdc3-4057-5d74-97aa-8279dd26fa33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356091} :answer-field/id #uuid "73d73bcd-8256-5970-b6ac-230469df04c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e058ddbe-425b-5426-85a0-6f69cb521a44" :question/math-academy-id "q-89027" :question/problem "John keeps his marbles in two boxes, box $A$ and box $B.$ Of the marbles in his collection, $\\frac{3}{4}$ of them are in box $A,$ and $\\frac{1}{4}$ of them are in box $B.$ If $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the marbles in box $A$ are red and $\\frac{3}{5}$ of the marbles in box $B$ are red, what is the probability that a randomly selected marble in John's collection is red?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that the chosen marble is red, and let $A$ be the event that the marble was selected from box $A.$ Then, ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the marble was selected from box $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected marble is red, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(R)=P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $\\frac{3}{4}$ of the marbles are in box $A$ and $\\frac{1}{4}$ are in box $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{3}{4},\\,P({A}^{′})=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the marbles in box $A$ are red and $\\frac{3}{5}$ of the marbles in box $B$ are red, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|A)=\\frac{1}{3},\\,P(R|{A}^{′})=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected marble is red is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3})(\\frac{3}{4})+(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{5+3}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0f7055d-9f45-5115-97f4-35d0c56008e0" :knowledge-point/title "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2566a906-660a-5539-8fd2-506a252beb2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.637" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354977} {:answer/id #uuid "357ee747-8ccf-5b3a-ad24-9fe9d1620072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.579" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355146} {:answer/id #uuid "494958d9-9794-5e66-b1da-63cf9ff33c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.623" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355311} {:answer/id #uuid "654869f5-50fd-5413-ada4-6ad56a42a98b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.518" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355563} {:answer/id #uuid "e463b964-6231-5f45-aec0-6341c1449646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.535" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "357ee747-8ccf-5b3a-ad24-9fe9d1620072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.579" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355146} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f0e9367-128c-5d29-9e01-6dd7b04cb3fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357202}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc55985a-a406-5bf4-8dc7-f8f925865628" :question/math-academy-id "q-89040" :question/problem "In a gas station, $42%$ of the clients buy regular gasoline while $58%$ buy premium gasoline. If $62%$ of the clients that buy regular gasoline paid with a credit card while $55%$ of the clients that buy premium gasoline paid with a credit card, what is the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be the event that the chosen person paid with a credit card, and let $R$ be the event that the person bought regular gasoline. Then ${R}^{′}$ is the event that the person bought premium gasoline.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person paid with a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $42%$ of the clients buy regular gasoline while $58%$ buy premium gasoline, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.42,\\,P({R}^{′})=0.58.\n$$\n\n- Since $62%$ of the clients that buy regular gasoline paid with a credit card while $55%$ of the clients that buy premium gasoline paid with a credit card, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|R)=0.62,\\,P(C|{R}^{′})=0.55.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′}) \\\\  & =(0.62)(0.42)+(0.55)(0.58) \\\\  & ≈0.579\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5000a4b1-c0cb-59f3-9d45-6287fa8ac9b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7acda339-8e7a-5d97-8d07-87d56f55cf59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355742} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac1d3b7-75a1-5c1c-8ff2-b5129406317f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356039} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd7d09b-b5f0-5551-884a-1cbb0b928521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356071} {:answer/id #uuid "c42b2c84-13f4-5082-b461-631d5e9b5fcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356376} {:answer/id #uuid "e20a7c7c-2570-5ddb-b5bf-ae180319b6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c42b2c84-13f4-5082-b461-631d5e9b5fcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356376} :answer-field/id #uuid "20d03e9b-4d9b-5f17-8196-2161f649cd18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e146c7f5-1f60-52aa-a388-c884e771fa07" :question/math-academy-id "q-89044" :question/problem "Jacob has two email accounts, account $A$ and account $B.$ In account $A$ he receives $40%$ of his emails and he receives the remaining $60%$ in account $B.$ Approximately $30%$ of the emails received in account $A$ are spam and $36%$ of the emails received in both accounts are spam. What is the probability that a randomly selected email from account $B$ is spam? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be the event that the chosen email is spam, and let $A$ be the event that the email selected is from account $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the email selected is from account $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected email is spam, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S|A)P(A)+P(S|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40%$ of the emails are received in account $A$ and the remaining $60%$ in account $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.40,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.60.\n$$\n\n- Since $30%$ of the emails received in account $A$ are spam, we have \n\n$$\nP(S|A)=0.30.\n$$\n\n- Since $36%$ of the emails received in both accounts are spam, we have \n\n$$\nP(S)=0.36.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(S|{A}^{′})$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S|A)P(A)+P(S|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.36 & =(0.30)(0.40)+P(S|{A}^{′})(0.60) \\\\ 0.36 & =0.12+0.6P(S|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.6P(S|{A}^{′}) & =0.24 \\\\ P(S|{A}^{′}) & =0.40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected email from account $B$ is spam is $0.40.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5000a4b1-c0cb-59f3-9d45-6287fa8ac9b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13ec3618-ccd9-584a-a671-2d5604a02992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354840} {:answer/id #uuid "35323479-c980-5e35-8a43-4987fdb1d440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355140} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac8d006-37eb-5ce9-b169-71af759ce8a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355741} {:answer/id #uuid "836c2510-2c98-5336-8916-b05ea6092d1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355823} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5dcc0b-b087-57d3-a0dd-557c5d8b3697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b5dcc0b-b087-57d3-a0dd-557c5d8b3697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356042} :answer-field/id #uuid "7424e924-2e89-51e9-90d0-aa5b63cbb997" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a852c2d-1240-54fc-a444-5143c0301a19" :question/math-academy-id "q-89051" :question/problem "Daniel has a desk with two drawers, drawer $A$ and drawer $B.$ Of the total amount of pens that he has, $\\frac{4}{5}$ of them are in drawer $A,$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$ are in drawer $B.$ Also, $\\frac{3}{4}$ of the pens in drawer $A$ are blue, and Daniel knows that $\\frac{2}{3}$ of the pens in both drawers are blue. What is the probability that a randomly selected pen from drawer $B$ is blue?" :question/worked-solution "Let $U$ be the event that the chosen pen is blue, and let $A$ be the event that the pen was selected from drawer $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the pen was selected from drawer $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected pen is blue, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(U)=P(U|A)P(A)+P(U|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $\\frac{4}{5}$ of the pens are in drawer $A$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$ are in drawer $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{4}{5},\\,P({A}^{′})=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{3}{4}$ of the pens in drawer $A$ are blue, we have\n\n$$\nP(U|A)=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{2}{3}$ of the pens on the desk drawers are blue, we have\n\n$$\nP(U)=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(U|{D}^{′})$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(U) & =P(U|A)P(A)+P(U|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & =(\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{4}{5})+P(U|{A}^{′})(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & =\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}P(U|{A}^{′}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}P(U|{A}^{′}) & =\\frac{10-9}{15} \\\\ P(U|{A}^{′}) & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected pen from drawer $B$ is blue is $\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172023 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e6159dd-c12e-5882-be17-815b4cc9ae52" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Radical Function With an Odd Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "720ce82d-536f-52e4-a924-9dc9994eace0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355669} {:answer/id #uuid "9c357947-0335-531e-b792-b57594efd6eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356049} {:answer/id #uuid "a14eace9-3890-5be9-b73b-56227bff4efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356096} {:answer/id #uuid "a60dca9c-200d-526d-95e8-ae5ecfbe0621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356135} {:answer/id #uuid "d317e9da-0cf9-523f-af7e-fdda914c3077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no roots" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a14eace9-3890-5be9-b73b-56227bff4efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356096} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a0524cb-a296-51c4-96c8-ba5fc5980ac8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1da47db3-4817-571a-b409-df8d62fa2bb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-92875" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{5x+12}-3.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{5x+12}-3=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{5x+12}-3 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{5x+12} & =3 \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{5x+12})^{3} & ={3}^{3} \\\\ 5x+12 & =27 \\\\ 5x & =15 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115205 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7df2ec3-6138-5b84-864b-a856c28b9747" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Intercept of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "110202e8-093f-58a8-9e0d-f6d0462fe4fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354817} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec7982b-e6e2-5127-9750-dd495e4fc60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5e+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355367} {:answer/id #uuid "6b5cd5d7-65b0-52b7-a3d8-f1ee2974f09d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355615} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8dd3e6-8366-51e5-8487-6e2a2ed44084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $y$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355616} {:answer/id #uuid "bd971419-1d83-5381-8060-ebd02a942d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd971419-1d83-5381-8060-ebd02a942d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356325} :answer-field/id #uuid "388f326d-f720-5ae1-b8d8-e7be69c7f1e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab1d8b5d-ee01-5c7e-86ea-f76938391b95" :question/math-academy-id "q-92930" :question/problem "What is the $y$-intercept of $y=-5{e}^{-{x}^{3}}+3?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5{e}^{-(0)^{3}}+3 \\\\  & =-5{e}^{0}+3 \\\\  & =-5(1)+3 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115204 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c2518d5-0fde-5dfc-aa07-f1bdd3c1585a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the End-Behavior of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3346b133-465c-5f7c-8f6e-ed203cfa9c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355115} {:answer/id #uuid "6e902916-33fe-5cad-9fa1-d693583d5c07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355642} {:answer/id #uuid "7016e321-e75a-5e1d-9c03-93b9317abb94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355653} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef13b5a-fed1-5020-9287-b841ede6880e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355925} {:answer/id #uuid "e3296fe7-7c8e-558c-bc20-fd9a8725aecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e902916-33fe-5cad-9fa1-d693583d5c07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355642} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd144ebe-3889-52dd-aeed-40781f7c5ac5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e35f675f-b059-5f5e-9a19-8ac6f6c3e6e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-92963" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x-3}+2.$ Given that $f(x)\\to a$ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ then $a=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/86/8646efab1e707e57bd8ff93b5f3f7b1336ae23588abf2d423a45bbcf702f86ab.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=2$ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 2\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115204 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c2518d5-0fde-5dfc-aa07-f1bdd3c1585a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the End-Behavior of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42ba6113-d9e6-554c-a5ab-303df960703d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355256} {:answer/id #uuid "59222bb1-9b57-550e-b74f-18960c6bcc76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355454} {:answer/id #uuid "b9602130-4235-5679-81e3-ffa15cdfc320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356288} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c96c1e-b278-534e-bfd6-d6d031552911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356557} {:answer/id #uuid "e710d78c-3d06-5abd-ba31-927a221020a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42ba6113-d9e6-554c-a5ab-303df960703d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355256} :answer-field/id #uuid "195e4818-64d5-542b-b720-a7f4bc8b73d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43f0b9fb-d57d-5094-ba73-d0cc6a85c5c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-92988" :question/problem "Find the equation of the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=2({e}^{2x})-5.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/fc/fc2af8bae74f163f0dd4eae74fd90fc6c5460cae552c7e5072c83521444aea29.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=-5$ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we conclude that the equation of the horizontal asymptote is $y=-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115203 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee02c223-dd7f-5c5d-b09f-e7bc6bd16b0c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Root of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15eaa20a-798d-591d-9791-7bfbe7399837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354865} {:answer/id #uuid "3d31328a-39b6-52ea-807f-d59024fad9ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355210} {:answer/id #uuid "4560de0c-70f4-51f5-a9a3-63faf56a8b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355280} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d7c148-b989-57f1-b1ac-86ad209009b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356254} {:answer/id #uuid "e7836302-9e53-590b-81c4-7e3b4b7e7bce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4560de0c-70f4-51f5-a9a3-63faf56a8b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355280} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d1ba9a3-81a9-5dc9-9598-2c7b40e1e779" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1738a08a-0e6c-5ca9-b9ee-772359397eb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-92995" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)={e}^{x}-{e}^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}-{e}^{4}=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding ${e}^{4}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-{e}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}-\\cancel{{e}^{4}}+\\cancel{{e}^{4}} & =0+{e}^{4} \\\\ {e}^{x} & ={e}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $e$, we equate the exponents. This gives\n\n$$\nx=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115203 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee02c223-dd7f-5c5d-b09f-e7bc6bd16b0c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Root of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "024cb00d-23a5-5998-a7e2-0ef6f494abe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354676} {:answer/id #uuid "35ac62bd-1fa9-5ff6-bc3a-d5415133874f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355149} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff56156-dec0-5625-887c-a6a00810f413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355376} {:answer/id #uuid "5045dc71-23e2-5c2f-aeb5-cf26fed94158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355380} {:answer/id #uuid "d787633f-7310-59a3-a889-9935ea02f4fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d787633f-7310-59a3-a889-9935ea02f4fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356550} :answer-field/id #uuid "742646f8-b0b0-5de6-aa67-6e561c90e6e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dc356c61-c382-5786-b505-51f9c59fb0a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-92998" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=4{e}^{2x-1}-8.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n4{e}^{2x-1}-8=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding $8$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2x-1}-8 & =0 \\\\ 4{e}^{2x-1}-\\cancel{8}+\\cancel{8} & =0+8 \\\\ 4{e}^{2x-1} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2x-1} & =8 \\\\ {e}^{2x-1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x-1} & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ 2x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc94085c-4088-5eaf-887a-eea7f040573c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Z-Score of a Given Observation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "268ee764-f4ba-5bb4-b9a6-25d0217bbe25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354988} {:answer/id #uuid "46057ddc-18e7-5373-90a9-8e327b81e327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355286} {:answer/id #uuid "9818d993-5599-51c2-be03-5470aa4ffc0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356007} {:answer/id #uuid "99b6d8a4-b90d-5fc6-aad9-00a4aaa9f246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356029} {:answer/id #uuid "e518197b-b17c-5f90-b633-521d029c1507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46057ddc-18e7-5373-90a9-8e327b81e327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355286} :answer-field/id #uuid "23bf6b9f-ca8a-544b-9d4e-a0a158f07d3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e38779e8-5306-5b5e-a170-45db52e7f21d" :question/math-academy-id "q-94457" :question/problem "A data set has mean $\\overset{―}{x}=6$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=10.8.$ Find the $z$-score of an observation $x=10.5,$ giving your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overset{―}{x}=6,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{10.8}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=10.5$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & ≈\\frac{10.5-6}{\\sqrt{10.8}} \\\\  & ≈1.37\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc94085c-4088-5eaf-887a-eea7f040573c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Z-Score of a Given Observation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "202f2ede-33d8-5d23-a897-b75e4b1cc459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7.38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354933} {:answer/id #uuid "4ccb96c7-be37-57f3-af85-a3d9641697ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355348} {:answer/id #uuid "4e12e047-e3d4-5a77-974e-be93c594e33c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355357} {:answer/id #uuid "b276c159-4f0a-5ba8-bcf1-27b7f26ed839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356235} {:answer/id #uuid "baadf541-5610-5499-940a-702e8f2b4f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "202f2ede-33d8-5d23-a897-b75e4b1cc459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7.38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354933} :answer-field/id #uuid "beb393c6-1b84-5f14-9647-ac0b4335c2a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "694368b5-e718-5efc-b12c-37e7e99e29bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-94460" :question/problem "A data set has mean $\\overset{―}{x}=28$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=3.60.$ Find the $z$-score of an observation $x=14,$ giving your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overset{―}{x}=28,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{3.60}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=14$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & ≈\\frac{14-28}{\\sqrt{3.60}} \\\\  & ≈-7.38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083227 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2169053d-78fc-5714-82f4-d89fc36ff695" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Observation, Standard Deviation, or Mean Given a Z-Score"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2bcca9d-4ee4-558c-ac6d-3dba6fed8a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356239} {:answer/id #uuid "d8efc532-df6e-5410-bc77-a7e5779ef469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356568} {:answer/id #uuid "db1c6fb0-f620-582c-930f-ddf023b658c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356592} {:answer/id #uuid "e8af3748-e949-5d0a-b0f5-6cde72841fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356698} {:answer/id #uuid "f9242971-1fcf-5df3-b81b-b36d9bd54094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8efc532-df6e-5410-bc77-a7e5779ef469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356568} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f71beb9-8e13-54e1-a94f-41a8e740e60f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "530121a9-23c4-5834-af6f-63e88a6adfa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94471" :question/problem "A data set has a mean $\\overset{¯}{x}=14.5$ and an unknown standard deviation. An observation of the data set has the value $x=20$ and $z$-score $z=2.2.$ Find the standard deviation of the data set." :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=20,$ $\\overset{¯}{x}=14.5,$ and $z=2.2$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 20 & =14.5+2.2\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 5.5 & =2.2\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7e9daa2-4aaa-5c33-b611-231f3d0e4782" :knowledge-point/title "Z-Score: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1faf19fd-0dd5-586e-926d-6340b195ba2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354930} {:answer/id #uuid "49216ee7-3d5e-58e3-a5fb-5cdd2a0d69f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.0\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355310} {:answer/id #uuid "8b6197b1-b81b-52af-b216-72cc6a553860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.2\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355897} {:answer/id #uuid "d597713f-c6b1-5820-b22a-210e4633ed35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356535} {:answer/id #uuid "e81a51df-4cdd-5475-bfa1-e5313586a540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.0\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1faf19fd-0dd5-586e-926d-6340b195ba2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354930} :answer-field/id #uuid "366f682e-3735-536c-b4a3-11af61adcace" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1fa2412f-8859-5e81-9fb7-100702a88998" :question/math-academy-id "q-94519" :question/problem "The mean weight of a group of ballet dancers is $45\\text{ kg}.$ One of the dancers in the ballet group weighs $38\\text{ kg}$, and her $z$-score is $-2.5.$ Find the standard deviation of the weight of the dancers in the ballet group." :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=38,$ $\\overset{―}{x}=45,$ and $z=-2.5$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 38 & =45+(-2.5)\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ -7 & =-2.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{-7}{-2.5} \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the standard deviation of the ballet dancer's weights is $2.8\\,\\text{kg}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7e9daa2-4aaa-5c33-b611-231f3d0e4782" :knowledge-point/title "Z-Score: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19447168-1474-5be9-aac6-ad97fed12bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360937} {:answer/id #uuid "1fe70794-f488-59e6-afbe-8f9bdce41f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360972} {:answer/id #uuid "26497c17-1e52-5f13-9672-7d7541e882bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361001} {:answer/id #uuid "2e772d5a-11ce-5758-badf-fef6f326e8c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361044} {:answer/id #uuid "ed84d4c8-52d6-5fed-ade3-9113944fe3ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fe70794-f488-59e6-afbe-8f9bdce41f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360972} :answer-field/id #uuid "a007c32a-4b84-5c4e-9daf-279e1005a6e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a38c82cd-ff4b-53d8-85bc-f102ff90d451" :question/math-academy-id "q-9785" :question/problem "The mean weight of a group of newborn children is $3.2kg$ with a standard deviation of $350g.$\nWhat is the $z$-score that corresponds to a weight of $3kg?$ Give your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $350g=0.350kg$\n\nThe $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{¯}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3.2}{0.350} \\\\  & =-0.57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nto $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52e11164-1929-594d-9f9e-460f6469421d" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Stretched Negative Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc019953-ecf5-53ce-9d3b-5be9dc928b8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/76/76d779da22082439eb40586ddbeb325368b1f49f15fef3158ebaa0eee90f4d84.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395365} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a9fcde-d13a-59ef-9cbd-e86e49dc3376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/i